{"causes": ["Uzana's birth as a royal prince of the Pagan dynasty positioned him for succession to the throne.", "His father, King Kyaswa's, abdication in 1251 directly led to Uzana assuming the kingship.", "The internal political instability and weakening central authority within the Pagan Empire contributed to the challenges of his reign."], "effects": ["Uzana's death in 1256 directly led to the ascension of his son, Narathihapate, to the Pagan throne.", "His brief and largely ineffective reign further exacerbated the internal weaknesses of the Pagan Empire.", "The circumstances of his death by a hunting accident contributed to the narrative of a king detached from governance."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "kyaswa-of-pagan", "sourceName": "Kyaswa of Pagan", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "uzana-of-pagan", "targetName": "Uzana of Pagan", "context": "Uzana of Pagan succeeded his father, Kyaswa, as king of the Pagan dynasty in 1251."}, {"sourceSlug": "uzana-of-pagan", "sourceName": "Uzana of Pagan", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "narathihapate", "targetName": "Narathihapate", "context": "Uzana's death in 1256 directly caused the succession of his son, Narathihapate, to the Pagan throne."}, {"sourceSlug": "uzana-of-pagan", "sourceName": "Uzana of Pagan", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "decline-of-pagan-empire", "targetName": "Decline of Pagan Empire", "context": "Uzana's reign from 1251 to 1256 occurred during the accelerating decline of the Pagan Empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "pagan-empire", "sourceName": "Pagan Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "uzana-of-pagan", "targetName": "Uzana of Pagan", "context": "The Pagan Empire contained the entire historical period of Uzana's kingship and life."}, {"sourceSlug": "bagan-myanmar", "sourceName": "Bagan, Myanmar", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "uzana-of-pagan", "targetName": "Uzana of Pagan", "context": "Uzana ruled from the royal city of Pagan (Bagan) and died in its vicinity in 1256."}], "places": [{"name": "Pagan, Myanmar", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Bagan, Myanmar", "role": "Site of Death"}, {"name": "Burma, Southeast Asia", "role": "Kingdom Ruled"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Uzana of Pagan", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "uzana-of-pagan"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3129686", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uzana_of_Pagan"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Uzana's brief reign as king of Pagan from 1251 to 1256, though not marked by major events, is historically significant as it represents a critical transitional period of accelerating internal decay preceding the final collapse of the Pagan Empire and the subsequent Mongol invasions.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-30T07:06:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30521406406", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Uzana was king of Pagan dynasty of Burma (Myanmar) from 1251 to 1256. He assumed the regnal name "Śrī Tribhuvanāditya Dhammarājajayasūra" (ၐြီတြိဘုဝနာဒိတျဓမ္မရာဇဇယသူရ).", "newValue": "Uzana, who reigned as king of the Pagan dynasty of Burma (Myanmar) from 1251 to 1256, ascended to the throne under the regnal name "Śrī Tribhuvanāditya Dhammarājajayasūra" (ၐြီတြိဘုဝနာဒိတျဓမ္မရာဇᱡယသူရ). His brief five-year tenure marked a period of increasing internal fragility within the Pagan Empire, a once-dominant Buddhist kingdom in Southeast Asia. As the son and successor of King Kyaswa, Uzana inherited a kingdom already showing signs of decline, characterized by a weakening central authority and the growing power of local chieftains and religious institutions. His reign is often viewed as a transitional phase leading directly to the ultimate collapse of the dynasty a few decades later.

Uzana's rule was largely uneventful in terms of grand conquests or significant reforms, primarily due to its brevity and the pre-existing internal strife. He spent much of his time away from the capital, reportedly indulging in hunting, a pastime that ultimately contributed to his demise. The most significant event during his reign was the growing influence and eventual rebellion of his own son, Narathihapate, who would later become the last effective king of Pagan. This dynastic instability, a hallmark of the mid-13th century Pagan, saw royal authority diminish as regional lords and the sangha (Buddhist monastic order) accrued considerable land and power, further decentralizing the state.

Uzana's death in 1256, purportedly from a hunting accident near the city of Bagan, paved the way for the tumultuous reign of Narathihapate. While Uzana himself did not instigate the empire's downfall, his inability to consolidate power or reverse the centrifugal forces at play ensured that the Pagan state continued its trajectory towards disintegration. His reign thus serves as a critical, albeit minor, chapter in the narrative of Pagan's decline, directly preceding the era of Mongol incursions that would irrevocably alter the political landscape of Burma."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-30T07:06:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30521406406", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1213-03-02, Bagan"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "1213-03-02, Bagan"}], "newValue": ["Uzana's birth as a royal prince of the Pagan dynasty positioned him for succession to the throne.", "His father, King Kyaswa's, abdication in 1251 directly led to Uzana assuming the kingship.", "The internal political instability and weakening central authority within the Pagan Empire contributed to the challenges of his reign."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-30T07:06:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30521406406", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1256-05-08"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1256-05-08"}], "newValue": ["Uzana's death in 1256 directly led to the ascension of his son, Narathihapate, to the Pagan throne.", "His brief and largely ineffective reign further exacerbated the internal weaknesses of the Pagan Empire.", "The circumstances of his death by a hunting accident